{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon is set to reverse its pioneering drug decriminalization policy as a new law, HB4002, takes effect. This law reintroduces criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth, making personal-use possession a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law offers individuals caught with illicit drugs a choice: face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. This approach aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and imposes harsher penalties for selling drugs near public spaces like parks.\n\n***\n\nProponents of HB4002 argue that its flexible framework allows counties to tailor treatment alternatives based on their resources. Critics, however, warn that this could result in an inconsistent and inequitable application of policy across different regions. Strict criteria must be met for deflection, including no outstanding charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, and medical stability.\n\n***\n\nPortland's police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the tight 30-minute window for a mobile behavioral health outreach team to make contact after eligibility is determined. Failure to meet this timeline results in the individual being taken to jail. The process involves officers contacting a county dispatch line to assess eligibility before connecting the individual with support services.\n\nP.P.S. As this policy shift occurs across the country, considerations of age and individual circumstance will be crucial. For many, the struggle with addiction can affect all aspects of life, from daily eating habits to the ability to go shopping without being distracted by craving. The law's im